{
  "query": "A global ticketing company that operates scrumtuous.com is moving its high volume booking platform to Google Cloud. The system must preserve strict transactional consistency, scale out during major releases to around 90,000 reservations per minute, and remain highly available across multiple regions. As the Cloud Database Engineer, which Google Cloud service should you choose to meet these needs?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Cloud Bigtable",
      "explanation": "This is a wide column NoSQL database that excels at petabyte scale and low latency streaming workloads",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Cloud Storage",
      "explanation": "This is durable object storage designed for unstructured data such as media files, archives, and backups",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Cloud Spanner",
      "explanation": "This is a globally distributed relational database that provides horizontal scalability with strong consistency and high availability",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "AlloyDB for PostgreSQL",
      "explanation": "This is a PostgreSQL compatible managed database that delivers high performance and enterprise capabilities for relational workloads",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Cloud Spanner</b>.</p><p><b>Cloud Spanner</b> is a fully managed relational database that delivers external consistency for transactions and supports multi region configurations with synchronous replication and automatic failover. This matches the need for strict transactional consistency and high availability across regions. It scales horizontally without manual sharding and can handle very high throughput, so it is well suited to spikes such as 90,000 reservations per minute.</p><p><i>Cloud Bigtable</i> is a wide column NoSQL database that does not provide multi row ACID transactions and its multi cluster replication is not strictly consistent across regions. This makes it a poor fit for a booking system that requires strong transactional guarantees.</p><p><i>Cloud Storage</i> is an object store and not a transactional database. It lacks transactions, relational querying, and low latency indexing, so it cannot meet the requirements of a high volume reservation platform.</p><p><i>AlloyDB for PostgreSQL</i> offers high performance PostgreSQL with regional high availability, yet the primary is regional and cross region replicas are read only. It does not provide globally distributed strongly consistent writes across regions, so it cannot satisfy the requirement for strict multi region transactional consistency.</p>",
